1
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,100
 Steam. You know, it wasn't so long ago that we learned how to harness the power of steam. Barely two centuries.

2
00:00:19,170 --> 00:00:26,990
 Joe, I remember you telling us that long ago in Alexandria, a man named Hero had already invented a steam machine. Wasn't that what you said?

3
00:00:27,430 --> 00:00:30,860
 Get ready for a big lesson on the Greeks.

4
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:33,580
 Alexandria wasn't in Greece. It's in Egypt.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,180
 That's right, but Hero's invention had no real application, no practical use. You remember, children.

6
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,320
 I invented a steam machine.

7
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:58,530
 It's of no use.

8
00:00:58,530 --> 00:01:00,540
 No, it's true. It served no useful purpose.

9
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:12,850
 You have to wait another 2,000 years before a Frenchman, Denis Papin, takes an interest once again in a steam machine.

10
00:01:18,330 --> 00:01:27,510
 It can generate a lot of power.

11
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:43,920
 There is great power.

12
00:01:45,710 --> 00:01:52,720
 I don't know if it has power, but I do know it's hot.

13
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:54,450
 To be careful, Monsieur Papin.

14
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:38,330
 Too slow, your fancy machine, Monsieur Papin.

15
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:46,460
 I'll make a bigger one.

16
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:09,680
 Devalve, that's what it needs.

17
00:03:11,470 --> 00:03:14,510
 Starting to cook now, Monsieur Papin.

18
00:03:14,890 --> 00:03:16,840
 I just invented something else.

19
00:03:17,570 --> 00:03:20,610
 In no time at all, these vegetables are going to cook.

20
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:26,720
 You concoct it now.

21
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:29,440
 Already done! I call it a pressure cooker.

22
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:31,970
 But you can't cook them in such a short time.

23
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:39,870
 Bravo, Monsieur Papin! For once you have invented something useful!

24
00:03:39,870 --> 00:03:41,270
 What?

25
00:03:41,270 --> 00:03:46,440
 And so it was, children, that that was all that Denis Papin left us, the pressure cooker, but things would change.

26
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:49,940
 In fact, it would be a veritable explosion in steam, you'll see.

27
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:59,660
 Explosion, technologically speaking, naturally.

28
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:09,710
 The first steam machine used industrially was invented by Newcombe in England.

29
00:04:09,710 --> 00:04:41,390
 Mr. Go on, we need more steam, go on.

30
00:04:41,390 --> 00:04:42,890
 Machine is uneconomical, I assure you.

31
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:43,830
 It wastes coal.

32
00:04:44,450 --> 00:04:45,910
 Go on, Professor Watt.

33
00:04:46,230 --> 00:04:47,070
 Back in Scotland.

34
00:04:47,390 --> 00:04:48,340
 We abhor waste.

35
00:04:48,590 --> 00:04:50,490
 See how much steam's escaping into the air.

36
00:04:50,870 --> 00:04:51,930
 We can do nothing.

37
00:04:52,670 --> 00:04:53,610
 Ah, yes we can.

38
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:58,250
 Can't see anything, it's too small.

39
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:00,910
 There's also no need to waste paper.

40
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:03,190
 Can't understand a thing.

41
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:04,460
 You'll see.

42
00:05:14,140 --> 00:05:15,490
 Maestro, will you explain?

43
00:05:15,690 --> 00:05:17,990
 Explain how the wheel is turned just using steam?

44
00:05:18,230 --> 00:05:20,950
 Because steam, all it does is saw.

45
00:05:21,130 --> 00:05:23,050
 Yeah, it's all right, I'll try to explain it.

46
00:05:23,410 --> 00:05:26,690
 Now, slow down at this speed, we can't understand anything.

47
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:36,720
 The steam comes into this sort of drawer that distributes it alternately to each side of the piston,

48
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:38,560
 pushing one way and then the other, and it moves.

49
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:43,560
 Does everybody understand?

50
00:05:44,270 --> 00:05:46,300
 Yeah, I understand.

51
00:05:46,930 --> 00:05:49,830
 That steam engine was to revolutionize the world.

52
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:55,140
 It was only the start.

53
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:58,480
 Oh, yes, soon others designed and built steam engines.

54
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:01,150
 A Frenchman, Francois Cugnot, for instance.

55
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:08,280
 You are convinced, Monsieur Cugnot, that your machine is able to transport this large cannon, that right?

56
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:09,000
 You will see.

57
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:30,120
 I would prefer you take it in through the door, sir, and not through the wall.

58
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:31,550
 I'm trying, I'm trying!

59
00:06:31,550 --> 00:06:32,540
 No!

60
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:35,740
 Fire the Englishman, Murdoch.

61
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:42,140
 It is not allowed to put the mechanical devices on the road during the day, Murdoch.

62
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:45,150
 Then I'll test it at night, Mr. Watt.

63
00:06:45,150 --> 00:06:46,880
 All right, but it's at your own risk.

64
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:54,460
 My goodness.

65
00:06:54,460 --> 00:07:39,170
 It's not a toy, it's a scale model of the very first locomotive on rails.

66
00:07:39,170 --> 00:07:44,000
 There's no machine that'll have a good horse.

67
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:59,290
 Just a few years later, in 1797...

68
00:07:59,290 --> 00:08:31,650
 Idea.

69
00:08:31,650 --> 00:08:37,410
 Just as well, hook an engine to the car and it would move on its own.

70
00:08:37,410 --> 00:08:42,160
 Been tried already, George. The machine is too heavy. It won't pull all the carts.

71
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:44,200
 And the rails break.

72
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:46,140
 Now what are you doing?

73
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:50,620
 Nothing, Pop. It's just the piston. It's stuck.

74
00:08:50,620 --> 00:08:52,860
 Drop of oil should do it.

75
00:08:52,860 --> 00:08:54,820
 George?

76
00:08:54,820 --> 00:08:56,250
 Hold on. It'll work.

77
00:08:56,250 --> 00:09:05,050
 Son, you're good at mechanics.

78
00:09:05,050 --> 00:09:07,870
 I want to construct a real locomotive.

79
00:09:07,870 --> 00:09:09,950
 For that, I'll be needing a workshop.

80
00:09:09,950 --> 00:09:13,640
 Range that.

81
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:21,510
 The first public train service running on rails would begin in 1803 in England and be pulled by horses.

82
00:09:22,910 --> 00:09:25,000
 Hey, that wasn't a real train.

83
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,380
 Has to have a loco--

84
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:28,450
 Yeah, you're right.

85
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:30,760
 Very soon, real locomotives would appear.

86
00:09:31,570 --> 00:09:31,830
 See?

87
00:09:42,870 --> 00:09:50,530
 Run, Dad.

88
00:09:51,250 --> 00:09:53,410
 Wow, but it's too heavy to run for long.

89
00:09:54,190 --> 00:09:56,260
 Are you familiar with steam engines?

90
00:09:56,600 --> 00:09:58,240
 Yes, sir. I'm a trained mechanic.

91
00:09:58,730 --> 00:10:04,840
 Well, my machine is going to run so fast, nobody will beat it in a race, so I named it Catch Me If You Can.

92
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:07,630
 Weighs about seven tons. Heavy for the rails.

93
00:10:08,440 --> 00:10:10,170
 Sure you, I know my job.

94
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:13,820
 Take a ride on the locomotive. It's the transport of the future.

95
00:10:13,820 --> 00:10:16,500
 One shilling a ride, one shilling, a little shilling.

96
00:10:17,150 --> 00:10:19,880
 Hello, Dad. Can I have a ride? A good time.

97
00:10:19,940 --> 00:10:22,100
 Yes, all right, but sit on the outer side.

98
00:10:22,340 --> 00:10:24,980
 I'm still afraid that the rails will give way, and then you can jump.

99
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:45,330
 Too heavy, huh?

100
00:10:45,630 --> 00:10:47,850
 What's needed is a much lighter machine, understand?

101
00:10:48,290 --> 00:10:51,050
 And so you want to build another locomotive, George?

102
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:55,330
 Yes, sir, but one that's much lighter and so faster.

103
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:56,540
 Just no word, my lord.

104
00:10:56,910 --> 00:11:01,170
 These locomotives are quite costly toys and clearly will never be any use.

105
00:11:01,420 --> 00:11:03,010
 Horses, now that's a good investment.

106
00:11:04,670 --> 00:11:06,430
 It's true, they cost a great deal.

107
00:11:07,070 --> 00:11:07,770
 Yes, my lord.

108
00:11:07,910 --> 00:11:08,430
 How much?

109
00:11:09,230 --> 00:11:11,660
 Many hundreds of pounds, perhaps a thousand.

110
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:13,770
 Out of the question! Out of the question!

111
00:11:14,010 --> 00:11:16,770
 And if you do manufacture one, you sure it'll run?

112
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:18,780
 Yes, sir. I guarantee it'll run.

113
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:20,580
 Don't listen to him, my lord.

114
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:24,040
 Canik, the man is not properly trained.

115
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:28,730
 Well, I please trust in you, George.

116
00:11:29,370 --> 00:11:31,130
 Here's what you need for a workshop.

117
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,550
 It's madness. I don't agree.

118
00:11:34,970 --> 00:11:36,590
 All that gold will be wasted.

119
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:50,070
 Wood is light, and it's going to strengthen the boiler, son.

120
00:11:50,310 --> 00:11:51,530
 Ah, look. Look over there.

121
00:11:51,530 --> 00:11:53,060
 You see the two cylinders?

122
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:56,940
 Well, the rods will transmit the movement of the pistons directly to the wheels.

123
00:11:57,140 --> 00:12:00,750
 But you, Robert, you have to go to school to be an engineer.

124
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:03,290
 That way, you and I can work together.

125
00:12:11,610 --> 00:12:24,250
 For work.

126
00:12:24,430 --> 00:12:26,340
 The accounts are in a terrible state.

127
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:28,700
 Salaries are too high, steel supplies too high,

128
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:30,300
 pairs to the forge too high.

129
00:12:30,360 --> 00:12:32,380
 That's not all damages caused by explosion.

130
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:35,660
 This is Bill's bill's bill.

131
00:12:35,660 --> 00:12:41,400
 As a result, she is.

132
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:42,940
 I call her the locomotion.

133
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:46,780
 With this engine, we're going to start the very first steam railway line.

134
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:47,840
 He's crazy.

135
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:49,450
 I second that.

136
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:51,030
 Then let's build it.

137
00:12:53,780 --> 00:13:00,460
 Are you sure, George, that your locomotive will be ready in time?

138
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:06,500
 Yes, quite sure. My son is in charge of the workshop. I have complete faith in him. The train will run.

139
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:09,460
 Yeah, yeah, it runs. It runs up bills, your lordship.

140
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:12,780
 We won't be needing them at all.

141
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:14,160
 I certainly hope not.

142
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:16,740
 This is going to be a great day in railroad history.

143
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:19,760
 It's wonderful. I want to be in front of you.

144
00:13:19,760 --> 00:13:21,000
 Me too. Move over here.

145
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:36,970
 You realize it's impossible.

146
00:13:37,420 --> 00:13:38,360
 Train won't run long.

147
00:14:18,280 --> 00:14:45,210
 Who's here?

148
00:16:01,240 --> 00:16:02,690
 Port of Liverpool by rail.

149
00:16:03,340 --> 00:16:07,010
 Question now is, who is going to construct this great project?

150
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:09,940
 It's already decided. I have drawn the route.

151
00:16:11,590 --> 00:16:14,890
 The motive is ready. All we need is the money needed to construct it.

152
00:16:15,070 --> 00:16:19,930
 In just one minute, steam engines aren't yet proven the train is safer when it's drawn by horses.

153
00:16:20,270 --> 00:16:23,130
 As it happens, I am able to supply good quality horses.

154
00:16:23,710 --> 00:16:26,360
 Mr. James, we've examined your project.

155
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:30,460
 We're not absolutely sure that the route you've chosen is necessarily the best,

156
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:32,890
 nor that your locomotive is able to pull the train.

157
00:16:33,660 --> 00:16:40,170
 What do you mean? My locomotive is the best in the world, and I assure you, the route I've chosen is the shortest one.

158
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:46,580
 To date, I believe the only steam-driven railway that's functioning is the one created by George Stevenson.

159
00:16:47,190 --> 00:16:48,530
 We have asked him to come.

160
00:16:50,450 --> 00:16:54,580
 Mr. Stevenson, and have you considered our project?

161
00:16:55,230 --> 00:16:57,860
 I have, and this is the route that I propose.

162
00:16:59,300 --> 00:17:02,780
 But the route he's made is much longer. It would be a waste of time.

163
00:17:02,780 --> 00:17:08,450
 It avoids steep grades and any sharp curbs along the route. They might derail the train.

164
00:17:08,800 --> 00:17:11,800
 Yes, of course. Now, what about the locomotive? What is your choice?

165
00:17:12,130 --> 00:17:15,080
 My opinion, it should be a new design, a boiler with pipes.

166
00:17:16,060 --> 00:17:16,890
 It's ridiculous.

167
00:17:17,780 --> 00:17:20,320
 The boiler design is cylindrical and it's a vertical.

168
00:17:20,750 --> 00:17:22,540
 No question, a horizontal boiler.

169
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:25,270
 My locomotive's the best in the world.

170
00:17:25,530 --> 00:17:27,170
 And mine is simply better.

171
00:17:27,670 --> 00:17:29,410
 Gentlemen, gentlemen, do stay calm.

172
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:36,320
 Each inventor thinks his is the best, yes, of course.

173
00:17:36,320 --> 00:17:38,800
 And so, there's only one way to settle this.

174
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:41,360
 That is a race.

175
00:17:42,220 --> 00:17:44,520
 That's an excellent idea.

176
00:17:45,940 --> 00:17:47,820
 A locomotive race?

177
00:17:48,180 --> 00:17:50,530
 With a prize of five hundred pounds sterling?

178
00:17:50,530 --> 00:17:52,960
 A race! We can all place bets!

179
00:17:52,960 --> 00:17:54,320
 When will it be?

180
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:56,380
 In October at Rainhill, near Liverpool.

181
00:17:56,380 --> 00:18:00,450
 And the minimum average speed has to be at least sixteen kilometres an hour.

182
00:18:07,540 --> 00:18:25,210
 To thank your honour for accepting an invitation.

183
00:18:25,210 --> 00:18:29,310
 This is going to begin.

184
00:18:29,310 --> 00:18:32,300
 Here are the judges, their decision is final.

185
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:34,660
 It can't be questioned.

186
00:18:34,660 --> 00:18:38,370
 Going forward, the first candidate.

187
00:18:38,370 --> 00:18:46,520
 Sir Adrian, I wonder how he powers it.

188
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:56,740
 That doesn't mean he qualifies a locomotive.

189
00:18:56,740 --> 00:19:02,080
 There's an hour.

190
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:06,200
 Mr. Brandreth, we believe that your engine is not powered by steam.

191
00:19:06,340 --> 00:19:08,140
 What? Not steam? You come and see.

192
00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:13,210
 Don't tell me it's not steam. It ain't powered by steam.

193
00:19:14,690 --> 00:19:21,160
 In a way, it is steam, yes, but in any event, your average speed, sir, was only 8 kilometers an hour.

194
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:25,680
 And the minimum for the race requires 16. Therefore, you are out.

195
00:19:26,230 --> 00:19:28,290
 Why didn't you tell me? I'd have brought two horses.

196
00:19:28,940 --> 00:19:29,990
 Next contestant.

197
00:19:31,350 --> 00:19:33,190
 Did it on stilts?

198
00:19:33,310 --> 00:19:35,130
 It looks quite like a grasshopper.

199
00:19:35,130 --> 00:19:38,050
 I don't think it will run for long, though.

200
00:19:38,050 --> 00:23:25,760
 For me, part of the glorious train saga, and before long, the next year, in fact,

201
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:28,830
 the Manchester-Liverpool line would go into service.

202
00:23:28,830 --> 00:23:32,990
 In just a few decades, the steam railway would undergo a remarkable development,

203
00:23:32,990 --> 00:23:37,210
 spreading through all of Europe and America, where it would open up the Wild West.

204
00:23:37,210 --> 00:23:39,330
 Where was I again?

205
00:23:39,330 --> 00:23:43,970
 That's right. Stevenson, with his rocket, had gone over 30 kilometers an hour.

206
00:23:43,970 --> 00:23:45,660
 And today...

207
00:23:45,660 --> 00:23:54,270
 J.V., you realize 300 an hour?

208
00:23:54,270 --> 00:23:57,920
 That's right. And pretty soon, it could be 500.

